WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.485 --> 00:00:04.318
- Energy.

00:00:04.318 --> 00:00:05.151
- We have the energy.

00:00:05.151 --> 00:00:07.391
- Sustain that sense of energy.

00:00:07.391 --> 00:00:08.224
- Great energy.

00:00:08.224 --> 00:00:09.057
- Energy.

00:00:09.057 --> 00:00:09.890
- Energy.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:11.580
- Hello Believe Nation, today
we're going to talk about

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.413
how you can raise your energy.

00:00:14.413 --> 00:00:15.650
And as always if you hear something

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:17.034
that really resonates with you

00:00:17.034 --> 00:00:18.620
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:19.854
and put quotes around it

00:00:19.854 --> 00:00:21.972
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:00:21.972 --> 00:00:22.805
Enjoy!

00:00:23.662 --> 00:00:27.829
(bright, triumphant orchestral music)

00:00:34.814 --> 00:00:37.033
- When I started Microsoft,

00:00:37.033 --> 00:00:38.798
I didn't think of it as all that risky.

00:00:38.798 --> 00:00:40.745
I mean, I was so excited
about what we were doing,

00:00:40.745 --> 00:00:43.830
it's true I could have gone bankrupt,

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.487
but you know, I had a set of skills

00:00:45.487 --> 00:00:47.281
that were highly employable,

00:00:47.281 --> 00:00:49.014
and in fact my parents were still willing

00:00:49.014 --> 00:00:52.126
to let me go back to Harvard
and finish my education

00:00:52.126 --> 00:00:53.489
if I wanted to.

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:54.742
- You've always got a job with me, Bill.

00:00:54.742 --> 00:00:56.704
(laughing)

00:00:56.704 --> 00:01:00.438
- And the thing that was
scary to me wasn't quitting

00:01:00.438 --> 00:01:01.727
and starting the company,

00:01:01.727 --> 00:01:03.397
it was when I started hiring my friends

00:01:03.397 --> 00:01:05.887
and they expected to be paid.

00:01:05.887 --> 00:01:09.254
(audience laughs)

00:01:09.254 --> 00:01:11.512
And then we had customers
who went bankrupt,

00:01:11.512 --> 00:01:13.939
customers who I'd counted
on to come through,

00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:17.040
and so then I got this
incredibly conservative approach

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.986
that I wanted to have
enough money in the bank

00:01:18.986 --> 00:01:21.247
to pay a year's worth of payroll,

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:25.073
even if we didn't get
any payments coming in.

00:01:25.073 --> 00:01:28.676
And I'm almost true to
that the whole time,

00:01:28.676 --> 00:01:29.776
we have about 10 billion now,

00:01:29.776 --> 00:01:32.144
which is pretty much
enough for the next year.

00:01:32.144 --> 00:01:34.378
(audience laughs)

00:01:34.378 --> 00:01:38.368
Anyway, you know, I, if you're
going to start a company

00:01:38.368 --> 00:01:40.007
it takes so much energy

00:01:40.007 --> 00:01:43.757
that it better overcome
your feeling of risk.

00:01:45.837 --> 00:01:49.137
I don't think that you necessarily,

00:01:49.137 --> 00:01:50.088
if you're going to start a company,

00:01:50.088 --> 00:01:51.451
should do it at the start of your career,

00:01:51.451 --> 00:01:52.437
I think there's a lot to be said

00:01:52.437 --> 00:01:53.617
for working for a company,

00:01:53.617 --> 00:01:55.139
learning how they do things,

00:01:55.139 --> 00:01:58.506
if you're young, it's
hard to go lease premises,

00:01:58.506 --> 00:02:00.090
they made that hard for me,

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.947
you couldn't rent a car when you were

00:02:01.947 --> 00:02:04.217
under 25 at the time,

00:02:04.217 --> 00:02:05.305
so I was always taking taxis

00:02:05.305 --> 00:02:06.828
to go see customers.

00:02:06.828 --> 00:02:08.793
(audience laughs)

00:02:08.793 --> 00:02:10.876
And the people would say,

00:02:12.402 --> 00:02:14.160
we're going to have a
discussion in the bar,

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:15.719
well I couldn't go to the bar.

00:02:15.719 --> 00:02:19.886
(audience laughing)
(audience applauds)

00:02:21.532 --> 00:02:24.670
But you know, that's fun
because I'll tell you,

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:26.867
when people are first skeptical,

00:02:26.867 --> 00:02:28.424
and they go this kid
doesn't know anything,

00:02:28.424 --> 00:02:31.648
then when you show them you've
really got a good product

00:02:31.648 --> 00:02:32.532
and you know something,

00:02:32.532 --> 00:02:33.706
they actually tend to go overboard

00:02:33.706 --> 00:02:36.832
and they think "Whoa, they know a lot!

00:02:36.832 --> 00:02:39.856
"Let's really do an incredible
amount with these people."

00:02:39.856 --> 00:02:42.321
So our youth, at least in this country,

00:02:42.321 --> 00:02:44.836
was a huge asset for us

00:02:44.836 --> 00:02:47.066
once we reached a certain threshold.

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:50.649
It is hard, it's hard
to hire older people,

00:02:51.517 --> 00:02:53.448
because they'll be a
little bit conservative

00:02:53.448 --> 00:02:56.943
about whether they should
come and take the risk.

00:02:56.943 --> 00:02:59.961
And it took three or four
years before we could go out

00:02:59.961 --> 00:03:02.447
into the normal sort of employment pool.

00:03:02.447 --> 00:03:04.657
But those problems come
with starting the firm.

00:03:04.657 --> 00:03:07.341
You better think of those as part

00:03:07.341 --> 00:03:09.540
of the pleasure, part of the challenge

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.555
that is part of the excitement.

00:03:12.555 --> 00:03:14.179
- And what do you think
the biggest difference

00:03:14.179 --> 00:03:15.624
between doing a play and doing,

00:03:15.624 --> 00:03:16.654
I mean it's a cliched question

00:03:16.654 --> 00:03:18.027
but I'd be interested to know your answer.

00:03:18.027 --> 00:03:19.815
- I think a part of what Viola just said,

00:03:19.815 --> 00:03:22.322
is that you get to dig deeper
and you get another day.

00:03:22.322 --> 00:03:24.416
And you get the energy.

00:03:24.416 --> 00:03:26.763
You do a movie, and it's 200
people that are used to it,

00:03:26.763 --> 00:03:28.913
and they don't care, and
everybody's doing their job

00:03:28.913 --> 00:03:30.678
and it comes in a theater somewhere,

00:03:30.678 --> 00:03:33.132
and you're picking your
nose at home somewhere,

00:03:33.132 --> 00:03:35.674
clipping your nails, there's no energy.

00:03:35.674 --> 00:03:38.792
When I was on Broadway five
years ago, Julius Caesar,

00:03:38.792 --> 00:03:41.508
I made a decision to sign
autographs every night

00:03:41.508 --> 00:03:44.367
I don't know what I did, it
ain't happening this time!

00:03:44.367 --> 00:03:48.490
(laughing)
(audience applauds)

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:49.706
Only, as I rub my knees,

00:03:49.706 --> 00:03:51.393
only because my knees are bad.

00:03:51.393 --> 00:03:52.520
It's harder for me to stand.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:53.353
But--

00:03:53.353 --> 00:03:54.970
- And why did you decide to do that?

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:56.675
- Well, you know, I
wanted to say thank you.

00:03:56.675 --> 00:03:58.517
It was my way of saying thank you.

00:03:58.517 --> 00:04:02.404
And the first night, it was
like, 5000 people out there

00:04:02.404 --> 00:04:03.482
so okay, wait a minute,

00:04:03.482 --> 00:04:05.599
everybody who has playbills from our show.

00:04:05.599 --> 00:04:07.269
(laughter)

00:04:07.269 --> 00:04:08.297
Yeah, because it was like,

00:04:08.297 --> 00:04:10.317
people were just wandering up the block.

00:04:10.317 --> 00:04:11.969
I'm like wait a minute, I forgot I'm on

00:04:11.969 --> 00:04:14.446
you know, 42nd street and Times Square.

00:04:14.446 --> 00:04:15.855
I said, wait a minute.
(audience laughing)

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:16.760
But honestly.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:18.799
- You were signing for weeks.
- The energy.

00:04:18.799 --> 00:04:20.925
I was out there hour and a
half, every night, every show.

00:04:20.925 --> 00:04:21.758
- [Ann] Wow.

00:04:21.758 --> 00:04:23.374
- But the energy you got back,

00:04:23.374 --> 00:04:24.583
the stories, the five little old ladies

00:04:24.583 --> 00:04:27.147
who just drove in from Detroit,

00:04:27.147 --> 00:04:29.131
and I'm like well what
you all doing tonight?

00:04:29.131 --> 00:04:30.462
They said "we getting
back in the car, baby,

00:04:30.462 --> 00:04:32.149
"we got to go back tonight."

00:04:32.149 --> 00:04:33.844
And you know, little old 84 year old lady

00:04:33.844 --> 00:04:36.491
was like, "baby if I was
three years younger."

00:04:36.491 --> 00:04:39.901
(audience laughing)
(audience applauds)

00:04:39.901 --> 00:04:41.645
Oh, true story!

00:04:41.645 --> 00:04:43.300
True story.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:44.185
Then we had,

00:04:44.185 --> 00:04:46.547
and I say this also,

00:04:46.547 --> 00:04:49.333
because I hope it happens with this play,

00:04:49.333 --> 00:04:51.408
we had some high schools come,

00:04:51.408 --> 00:04:53.461
we had one school from
somewhere, I forgot where

00:04:53.461 --> 00:04:55.837
and they were doing Julius Caesar.

00:04:55.837 --> 00:04:57.322
And some young kid was
like "well, you know,

00:04:57.322 --> 00:04:58.381
"my Brutus is a bit different."

00:04:58.381 --> 00:05:00.622
(audience laughs)

00:05:00.622 --> 00:05:02.623
"He's more the classic, stoic."

00:05:02.623 --> 00:05:04.504
But it was great to engage,

00:05:04.504 --> 00:05:07.673
and exchange with these young people,

00:05:07.673 --> 00:05:09.434
there was an energy.

00:05:09.434 --> 00:05:10.903
I got more from it than I gave

00:05:10.903 --> 00:05:13.413
to the people that I signed for.

00:05:13.413 --> 00:05:16.256
That's, you know, you don't get that,

00:05:16.256 --> 00:05:19.243
you know the odd thing
about success in film

00:05:19.243 --> 00:05:21.823
is that budgets get bigger,
you make more money,

00:05:21.823 --> 00:05:23.270
but they become more formulaic.

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:25.380
So you don't get the opportunity.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.820
So I, God willing, if I
stay around long enough,

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:29.942
this is what I'm going to be doing.

00:05:29.942 --> 00:05:31.942
- One of the things that

00:05:35.827 --> 00:05:38.077
people talk a lot about is,

00:05:38.934 --> 00:05:41.539
what gets someone to
become an entrepreneur?

00:05:41.539 --> 00:05:44.260
I mean I, it's just a
fascinating mindset, right?

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:46.879
You really have to be slightly delusional

00:05:46.879 --> 00:05:48.712
to be an entrepreneur,

00:05:49.597 --> 00:05:52.444
because you have to suspend disbelief.

00:05:52.444 --> 00:05:56.517
Or you have to believe about your own idea

00:05:56.517 --> 00:05:59.954
and have deep passion to go pursue it.

00:05:59.954 --> 00:06:01.288
So in some sense, quite honestly,

00:06:01.288 --> 00:06:03.833
before I even advise, I
take a lot of inspiration

00:06:03.833 --> 00:06:05.599
from what you're doing.

00:06:05.599 --> 00:06:07.203
It takes a lot of guts.

00:06:07.203 --> 00:06:11.532
It takes a lot of energy
to be an entrepreneur.

00:06:11.532 --> 00:06:12.895
And I think that

00:06:12.895 --> 00:06:17.243
therein lies, I think,
your secret to success.

00:06:17.243 --> 00:06:20.024
Which is your ability to overcome

00:06:20.024 --> 00:06:23.053
every constraint and every obstacle.

00:06:23.053 --> 00:06:27.220
And do it with the boldness,
and the risk-on nature of it.

00:06:29.885 --> 00:06:31.109
I think it's fantastic.

00:06:31.109 --> 00:06:35.696
If anything I would say,
especially for entrepreneurs,

00:06:35.696 --> 00:06:39.552
is, you got to sustain
that sense of energy

00:06:39.552 --> 00:06:41.286
over a long period of time

00:06:41.286 --> 00:06:45.315
because you all know, and
you are grounded in it.

00:06:45.315 --> 00:06:48.065
The mortality rate is super high.

00:06:48.923 --> 00:06:52.278
And that means you will
be thrown curveballs

00:06:52.278 --> 00:06:54.269
day in and day out,

00:06:54.269 --> 00:06:57.609
and you have to be able to overcome that.

00:06:57.609 --> 00:07:01.860
And so being a source of
energy in your small team,

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:03.682
especially if you're a CEO of a startup,

00:07:03.682 --> 00:07:05.421
I like to sort of absolve them a lot,

00:07:05.421 --> 00:07:08.566
because you can't have a bad day.

00:07:08.566 --> 00:07:09.517
You're all the time.

00:07:09.517 --> 00:07:11.282
You know you get up in the morning,

00:07:11.282 --> 00:07:12.843
you're calling in the afternoon,

00:07:12.843 --> 00:07:15.283
you're talking to your
investors in the evening,

00:07:15.283 --> 00:07:17.636
you're, ya know, hiring,

00:07:17.636 --> 00:07:20.832
and that constant sense of energy

00:07:20.832 --> 00:07:23.596
and passion for what you're doing

00:07:23.596 --> 00:07:25.585
is what I think it takes,

00:07:25.585 --> 00:07:26.978
and to never give up,

00:07:26.978 --> 00:07:29.660
which is what makes you who you are

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:31.697
in terms of the core makeup.

00:07:31.697 --> 00:07:36.401
And I think that for me, I am
personally inspired by that

00:07:36.401 --> 00:07:39.363
and I think that even
from a Microsoft culture,

00:07:39.363 --> 00:07:41.406
that's what really my dream is.

00:07:41.406 --> 00:07:42.826
My dream is not that, okay, we have

00:07:42.826 --> 00:07:45.035
you know, a large organization,

00:07:45.035 --> 00:07:47.623
my hope is that every
team inside of Microsoft

00:07:47.623 --> 00:07:49.622
feels both that sense of energy

00:07:49.622 --> 00:07:52.939
and empowerment and goes about it.

00:07:52.939 --> 00:07:54.740
- Do you have moments of repose,

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:58.745
moments of just, just take
it in and sit quietly?

00:07:58.745 --> 00:08:00.275
- No.

00:08:00.275 --> 00:08:02.166
(audience laughs)

00:08:02.166 --> 00:08:04.501
You know, I got to, I have
to do a lot of plane flights,

00:08:04.501 --> 00:08:06.584
and I, oh, I'm a handful.

00:08:08.342 --> 00:08:09.852
(audience laughs)

00:08:09.852 --> 00:08:11.671
I don't know, I haven't
made it in the tabloids

00:08:11.671 --> 00:08:13.463
as the bloke who's started

00:08:13.463 --> 00:08:16.434
the riots and stuff on the planes, yet.

00:08:16.434 --> 00:08:18.234
But I guarantee you it will happen.

00:08:18.234 --> 00:08:20.659
You know, you can't hang
your arm out the window,

00:08:20.659 --> 00:08:22.717
you can't stop and have a pee

00:08:22.717 --> 00:08:24.289
and look at the wildlife,

00:08:24.289 --> 00:08:26.796
you know, you're stuck in this thing

00:08:26.796 --> 00:08:29.906
for like sometimes 14 hours in a leg,

00:08:29.906 --> 00:08:33.370
you know what, going to America
is a 14 hour stint, mate,

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:35.565
and it's shocking awful to sit there

00:08:35.565 --> 00:08:37.536
and all the, you know, you can't,

00:08:37.536 --> 00:08:39.177
I like a good movie.

00:08:39.177 --> 00:08:41.663
But not five of them in a row.

00:08:41.663 --> 00:08:43.108
(audience laughs)

00:08:43.108 --> 00:08:45.281
It's like, and you know I don't read much,

00:08:45.281 --> 00:08:46.660
you know, I'm just.

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:47.493
- You don't read?

00:08:47.493 --> 00:08:48.919
How come you don't read?

00:08:48.919 --> 00:08:50.131
- Well, I do read, but not you know,

00:08:50.131 --> 00:08:51.893
not for 14 hours.

00:08:51.893 --> 00:08:53.483
(audience laughs)

00:08:53.483 --> 00:08:55.475
You know, a SurfMax
takes a good 20 minutes.

00:08:55.475 --> 00:08:57.140
(audience laughs)

00:08:57.140 --> 00:09:01.034
♫ To all the ladies in the
place with style and grace

00:09:01.034 --> 00:09:04.741
♫ Allow me to lace these
lyrical douches in your bushes

00:09:04.741 --> 00:09:07.370
♫ Who rock grooves and make
moves with all the mommies

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:09.492
♫ The back of the club, sippin' Moet

00:09:09.492 --> 00:09:10.367
♫ is where you'll find me

00:09:10.367 --> 00:09:12.943
♫ The back of the club, mackin'
hoes, my crew's behind me

00:09:12.943 --> 00:09:17.361
♫ Mad question askin', blunt
passin', music blastin'

00:09:17.361 --> 00:09:19.045
To me, I like the live stuff

00:09:19.045 --> 00:09:20.635
better than the stuff on the album

00:09:20.635 --> 00:09:22.884
because the stuff on the
album seems more relaxed.

00:09:22.884 --> 00:09:24.686
And I think you got to
have a certain tone,

00:09:24.686 --> 00:09:27.421
know what I'm saying, like
maybe don't get too excited,

00:09:27.421 --> 00:09:28.679
just chill, let it flow,

00:09:28.679 --> 00:09:31.329
but when you're on stage,
sweat pouring down,

00:09:31.329 --> 00:09:34.595
I'm a big one, too, sweat be drenching.

00:09:34.595 --> 00:09:36.939
I just be representing,
know what I'm saying,

00:09:36.939 --> 00:09:37.860
just doing my thing.

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:40.321
♫ Now they heard you blowin up like nitro

00:09:40.321 --> 00:09:41.958
♫ and they want to stick the knife

00:09:41.958 --> 00:09:43.807
♫ through your windpipe slow

00:09:43.807 --> 00:09:46.025
- Do you run out of energy,
emotionally or physically?

00:09:46.025 --> 00:09:46.951
- This is him not sleeping.

00:09:46.951 --> 00:09:49.833
- This is me on no sleep now.

00:09:49.833 --> 00:09:50.699
I don't.

00:09:50.699 --> 00:09:53.845
I can't express how
happy of a person I am,

00:09:53.845 --> 00:09:56.937
I can't express how inspiring

00:09:56.937 --> 00:10:00.104
and, I guess you could say, motivating

00:10:01.179 --> 00:10:04.262
my life and the people around me are.

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:06.711
- You know, things work in the moment,

00:10:06.711 --> 00:10:08.317
and then can die off,

00:10:08.317 --> 00:10:09.997
and then things can not work in one moment

00:10:09.997 --> 00:10:11.208
and then come to life,

00:10:11.208 --> 00:10:12.756
and if you have any wisdom whatsoever

00:10:12.756 --> 00:10:16.995
you know to follow wherever
there is great energy.

00:10:16.995 --> 00:10:20.115
And that's what I followed
with Denis when we did Enemy,

00:10:20.115 --> 00:10:22.074
I went, this guy has great energy.

00:10:22.074 --> 00:10:24.861
We vibe on each other, and
this, I got to go with this.

00:10:24.861 --> 00:10:26.983
Like, I don't know what
this movie's about,

00:10:26.983 --> 00:10:29.671
but I know this energy.

00:10:29.671 --> 00:10:33.120
And I know this energy
is success somewhere.

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:34.798
Creative success.

00:10:34.798 --> 00:10:36.904
And that's first and foremost to me.

00:10:36.904 --> 00:10:38.997
And that's what I follow now.

00:10:38.997 --> 00:10:40.504
And that energy drives a movie.

00:10:40.504 --> 00:10:42.194
When you watch as an
audience, it's infectious

00:10:42.194 --> 00:10:44.139
when you see that on a screen,

00:10:44.139 --> 00:10:46.101
and I see it all the time with people,

00:10:46.101 --> 00:10:49.407
you're infected by that energy.

00:10:49.407 --> 00:10:50.573
And that's what we want.

00:10:50.573 --> 00:10:52.313
- We average about 200,

00:10:52.313 --> 00:10:55.230
about 250 to 275 concerts per year,

00:10:58.939 --> 00:11:02.339
and sometimes that mean you
might do two in one day.

00:11:02.339 --> 00:11:05.672
I'm 69, I'll be 70 on September the 16th

00:11:08.112 --> 00:11:09.514
if I live that long.

00:11:09.514 --> 00:11:13.279
And I find the energy meeting
people like yourselves.

00:11:13.279 --> 00:11:14.529
I enjoy people.

00:11:15.392 --> 00:11:18.142
I love to play, see people happy.

00:11:19.941 --> 00:11:24.287
I love to see them dance and
see them enjoy themselves,

00:11:24.287 --> 00:11:26.089
that's energy for me.

00:11:26.089 --> 00:11:27.217
And it's educational.

00:11:27.217 --> 00:11:30.017
I get a chance to learn
how to say merci beaucoup.

00:11:30.017 --> 00:11:32.073
(laughs)

00:11:32.073 --> 00:11:35.058
I don't speak French, but Lucille does.

00:11:35.058 --> 00:11:36.963
Music is a universal language,

00:11:36.963 --> 00:11:40.045
and I believe that because
it's been many times

00:11:40.045 --> 00:11:41.752
I've heard great musicians,

00:11:41.752 --> 00:11:44.562
like Django Reinhardt I love,

00:11:44.562 --> 00:11:46.531
you never hear him speak English,

00:11:46.531 --> 00:11:49.129
but he speaks it with his guitar.

00:11:49.129 --> 00:11:51.632
So I think music is a universal language

00:11:51.632 --> 00:11:55.632
that we can, I believe,
communicate with anyone.

00:11:56.518 --> 00:12:00.016
- I think of energy, in its,
and anyone listening to this

00:12:00.016 --> 00:12:01.237
it's really important,

00:12:01.237 --> 00:12:03.894
when you start, the
way I did, with United,

00:12:03.894 --> 00:12:06.127
at 43, 44 years of age,

00:12:06.127 --> 00:12:09.185
you see the energy, the
players see the energy

00:12:09.185 --> 00:12:10.830
they see it bouncing out of you.

00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:14.997
but when you get to the 60s,
they still expect to see it.

00:12:16.901 --> 00:12:20.191
So I have to work at
myself in terms of energy.

00:12:20.191 --> 00:12:21.495
The hours I've slept,

00:12:21.495 --> 00:12:22.975
because when I was a young manager,

00:12:22.975 --> 00:12:25.357
four or five hours, no problem.

00:12:25.357 --> 00:12:27.635
When I got to that, to my 60s,

00:12:27.635 --> 00:12:30.541
I needed six or seven hours.

00:12:30.541 --> 00:12:34.575
So you go to bed earlier,
you work at your diet better,

00:12:34.575 --> 00:12:37.747
Saturday nights were out,
after the game went home,

00:12:37.747 --> 00:12:40.110
you know, I never, very very seldom

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:41.973
go out on a Saturday night.

00:12:41.973 --> 00:12:45.570
So that, I had to examine
myself as a manager

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:47.969
because people want to see the energy.

00:12:47.969 --> 00:12:50.246
It's a very important
aspect of it, you know.

00:12:50.246 --> 00:12:51.913
- Hands up, hands up

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:57.210
I always envisioned myself being a rapper

00:12:57.210 --> 00:12:59.210
and being in the game and having success,

00:12:59.210 --> 00:13:00.790
but you never know what
it really feels like

00:13:00.790 --> 00:13:02.646
or how you're going to
be when you're there.

00:13:02.646 --> 00:13:05.646
(audience cheering)

00:13:08.478 --> 00:13:09.311
Hands up!

00:13:13.606 --> 00:13:17.052
♫ And they say all I rap
about is bitches and champagne

00:13:17.052 --> 00:13:19.334
♫ You would too if every
night you seen the same thing

00:13:19.334 --> 00:13:21.486
The name Wiz comes from
me being the youngest dude

00:13:21.486 --> 00:13:25.230
in my age group of people
that I hung out with.

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:26.518
They would call me a young wiz,

00:13:26.518 --> 00:13:28.424
you know, like I was wise beyond my years,

00:13:28.424 --> 00:13:30.841
and the Khalifa is Arabic,
it means successor,

00:13:30.841 --> 00:13:33.329
leader, shining light, and my
granddad gave me that name.

00:13:33.329 --> 00:13:35.352
He's actually Muslim, so you know,

00:13:35.352 --> 00:13:37.510
he's just seeing my path
and what I was doing

00:13:37.510 --> 00:13:38.884
and how hard I was working,

00:13:38.884 --> 00:13:41.470
and he gave me that name, and
I just put the two together.

00:13:41.470 --> 00:13:43.637
(rapping)

00:13:51.083 --> 00:13:53.298
Of course, I do rap music
and I'm a hip hop artist,

00:13:53.298 --> 00:13:55.017
but as an individual,

00:13:55.017 --> 00:13:57.578
as a personality and what
I give to the people,

00:13:57.578 --> 00:13:58.811
I see myself as like,

00:13:58.811 --> 00:14:01.591
the lead singer of a band
or something. (laughs)

00:14:01.591 --> 00:14:04.374
You know, when I go out
there, I'm a real performer.

00:14:04.374 --> 00:14:05.894
It's all about the vibe and the feeling,

00:14:05.894 --> 00:14:07.130
you got to be balanced, and it's like,

00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:08.505
nothing that I have to put on,

00:14:08.505 --> 00:14:10.935
I really feel, you know, that energetic

00:14:10.935 --> 00:14:13.393
and that happy to be in front of the kids.

00:14:13.393 --> 00:14:14.839
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:14:14.839 --> 00:14:16.825
I'd love to know, what did you think?

00:14:16.825 --> 00:14:19.385
Did you get your energy raised
from watching this video?

00:14:19.385 --> 00:14:21.457
What was the most important
lesson that you learned

00:14:21.457 --> 00:14:22.960
that you want to apply to your life

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:24.433
or your business immediately,

00:14:24.433 --> 00:14:26.125
what's the first action step

00:14:26.125 --> 00:14:27.820
that you're going to take to apply it,

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:29.321
leave it in the comments below,

00:14:29.321 --> 00:14:30.528
super curious to find out.

00:14:30.528 --> 00:14:31.882
I also want to give a quick shout-out

00:14:31.882 --> 00:14:33.649
to BestYouPRO, thank you so much

00:14:33.649 --> 00:14:35.506
for picking up a copy of
my book "Your One Word"

00:14:35.506 --> 00:14:38.205
and taking that awesome picture
on Twitter and posting it,

00:14:38.205 --> 00:14:40.439
I really really really
really appreciate it.

00:14:40.439 --> 00:14:41.795
Thank you guys again for watching,

00:14:41.795 --> 00:14:44.904
I believe in you, I hope you
continue to believe in yourself

00:14:44.904 --> 00:14:48.028
and whatever your one word is, much love,

00:14:48.028 --> 00:14:49.611
I'll see you soon.

